# CASM Phase 12 WP68 Increment 7 trusted reference: casmarith2
#
# Independently hand-assembled from the casmarith2 fixture source in
# cmake/GenerateCasmTestFixtures.cmake. NOT produced by CASM. Proves the
# WP68 arithmetic/bitwise operators produce correct bytes when reached
# through the real production parser/emitter (parserParseExpressionValue,
# emitByteList, emitWordList) across every operand context that grammar
# reaches -- immediate, absolute, .BYTE, and .WORD -- plus one
# parenthesized RHS combining WP67 grouping with a WP68 operator. One
# representative operator per family (*, &, <<, unary -), per the WP68
# Increment 7 plan's Scoping Decision 1; per-operator numeric correctness
# across the full inventory is already closed by
# tests/src/casm_expr/casm_expr.s's own 97-case harness.
#
# Unary '-'/'~' both produce a full 16-bit two's-complement/complement
# result; a nonzero '-' result always carries a nonzero high byte, which
# ofRequire8Bit (opcodes.s) correctly rejects as
# CASM_DIAG_OPERAND_OUT_OF_RANGE for an immediate/.BYTE operand -- the
# same rule any other >255 literal already hits, not a bug -- this was
# discovered live (an earlier "LDA #-1" draft of this fixture correctly
# failed this way and was replaced). "LDA -1" (absolute, no '#')
# exercises unary '-' as an instruction operand instead, deliberately
# proving the WP68
# Increment 7 parser-dispatch fix: parser.s's outer parseOperandSequence
# whitelist (not just posImmediate's own inner one, which WP67 already
# extended for a leading '(') now accepts CASM_TOKEN_STAR/MINUS/TILDE as
# the first token of a non-immediate operand. "LDA #~$FF00" keeps unary
# '~' in immediate context with an input chosen so the complement's high
# byte is zero ($FF00 -> $00FF).
#
# Source (.ORG $C000, fixed address -- no relocatable symbols involved):
#   LDA #2*3            ; 2*3 = 6
#   LDA #$0F&$03         ; $0F & $03 = $03
#   LDA #1<<3            ; 1 << 3 = 8
#   LDA #~$FF00          ; ~$FF00 = $00FF, fits immediate
#   LDA -1               ; unary '-' two's complement = $FFFF, absolute
#   .BYTE 2*3, 1<<3      ; 6, 8
#   .WORD $0F&$03, (2+3)*2  ; $0003, $000A
#
# The first four LDA forms are immediate addressing ($A9, always 2 bytes)
# -- immediate mode has no zero-page/absolute width choice, so FORCE_ABS
# classification does not affect them (that proof is casmarithfwd's own
# job). "LDA -1" is 3-byte absolute ($AD lo hi) since $FFFF does not fit
# zero page.
#
# Hand assembly:
#   00 C0              PRG load-address header ($C000, little-endian)
#   A9 06              LDA #$06        (2*3)
#   A9 03              LDA #$03        ($0F&$03)
#   A9 08              LDA #$08        (1<<3)
#   A9 FF              LDA #$FF        (~$FF00 = $00FF, low byte)
#   AD FF FF           LDA $FFFF       (-1, absolute)
#   06 08              .BYTE 6, 8
#   03 00 0A 00        .WORD $0003, $000A
#
# bytes: 19
# sha256: 1cf95aeac017fa18ffbb7a44e59771298ac0bc56c7e153b8ab5b4d6d3c2d82cb
# source_sha256: casmarith2.seq=adf59be8a6940b2ba7d3c3ca6d481ba889f72e56cc99613a5d4efd39b4c5ae33

00 C0
A9 06
A9 03
A9 08
A9 FF
AD FF FF
06 08
03 00 0A 00
